{"id": "section-body-10087", "title": "Biliary Tract Diseases", "chapterId": "chapter-ten", "tocLevel": 4, "tocPath": ["APPROACH TO PATIENTS WITH EMERGENCY CONDITIONS", "GASTROINTESTINAL DISEASE CONDITIONS", "Disorders of The Liver and Biliary Tract", "Biliary Tract Diseases"], "blocks": [{"id": "body-10087", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Biliary Tract Diseases", "runs": [{"text": "Biliary"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Tract"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Diseases"}], "level": 4, "list": {"level": 2, "numberingId": "1460"}}, {"id": "body-10088", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Cholestasis is a pathologic state of reduced bile formation or flow which can be hepatocellular (Intrahepatic), where an impairment of bile formation occurs or ductular (extra hepatic), where impedance to bile flow occurs after it is formed. Intrahepatic causes of cholestasis include viral hepatitis, alcohol, primary biliary cirrhosis, drug toxicity, Hodgkin’s lymphoma and pregnancy. Extrahepatic causes include choledocholithiasis, carcinoma, and ascariasis of the biliary tree.", "runs": [{"text": "Cholestasis is a pathologic state of reduced bile formation or flow which can be hepatocellular (Intrahepatic), where an impairment of bile formation occurs or ductular (extra hepatic), where impedance to bile flow occurs after it is formed. Intrahepatic causes of cholestasis include viral hepatitis, alcohol, primary biliary cirrhosis, drug toxicity, Hodgkin’s lymphoma and pregnancy. Extrahepatic causes include choledocholithiasis, carcinoma, and ascariasis of the biliary tree."}]}, {"id": "body-10090", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Clinical Presentation", "runs": [{"text": "Clinical"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Presentation"}], "level": 4}, {"id": "body-10091", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "Jaundice,", "runs": [{"text": "Jaundice,"}], "list": {"level": 0, "numberingId": "1459"}}, {"id": "body-10092", "type": "paragraph", "